{"url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/", "final_url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/", "status": 200, "method": "http", "fetched": "2026-11-14", "text": "Welcome | Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nSkip to main content\nSearch\nGlobal Menu\nEnews Sign up\nTeam Portal\nMenu\nMain navigation\nAbout\nCaesar Kleberg\nCaesar Kleberg Foundation\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nAdvisory Board\nDevelopment Team\nScience Team\nOperations Team\nGraduate Students\nProspective Students\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nFire Ecology\nHabitat Management, Restoration & Research\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock & Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nQuail Associates 2.0\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas research Program\nRichard M. Kleberg Jr Center for Quail Research\nSouth Texas Natives\nTexas Native Seeds Program (TNS)\nWaterfowl and Wetland Birds\nWildlife Photography Program\nWildlife Diseases, Parasitology and Toxicology Research Program\nWildlife Center & Facilities\nAll Facilities\nTio and Janell Kleberg Wildlife Research Park\nCKWRI Ocelot Conservation Facility\nGiving\nMake a Donation\nCurrent Named Endowments\nNamed Giving Opportunities\nDevelopment Team\nGiving Forms\nPublications\nNews & Events\nMedia\nA Talk on the Wild Side Podcast\nSomewhere West of Wall Street\nWest of Texas\nEmployment\nContact\n\" >\nMake an Impact by Giving to an Endowment\nGive to an Endowment Now\nAll endowed funds are held in trust by the Texas A&M University-Kingsville Foundation (TAMUK), a 501 (c)3 charitable organization. By clicking the link, you will be taken to the TAMUK Foundation giving page. Please enter the name or keyword of the endowment you would like to support in the \"Find Your Fund Designation\" box.\nDONATE TO\nCKWRI\nDonate Now\n\" >\nComing Soon! CKWRI OCELOT CONSERVATION FACILITY\nDonate and learn more\nThe CKWRI Ocelot Conservation Facility will provide solutions to several needs for ocelot conservation and recovery throughout South Texas. The facility will provide the knowledge and capacity for researchers to produce ocelots for reintroduction and to understand the intricacies of ocelot behavior and health.\nDONATE TO\nCKWRI\nDonate Now\n\" >\nLearn about the Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nWatch Video\nThe Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute at Texas A&M University-Kingsville is the leading wildlife research organization in Texas and one of the finest in the nation. Our mission is to provide science-based information for enhancing the conservation and management of Texas wildlife.\nDONATE TO\nCKWRI\nDonate Now\n\" >\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute at Texas A&M University-Kingsville\nAbout CKWRI\nThe Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute's mission is to provide science-based information for enhancing the conservation and management of wildlife in South Texas and related environments.\nDONATE TO\nCKWRI\nDonate Now\nPreviousNext\nProviding the science behind wildlife conservation and management.\nWelcome\nFeatured News\nCan I Improve Antler Size of my Deer by Culling Bucks?\nStudies conducted in pens at the Kerr Wildlife Management Area by Texas Parks and Wildlife Department (TPWD) biologists led to recommendations that antler size could be improved by culling yearling (1.5-year-old) bucks. &nbsp;Spike bucks were first identified as genetically inferior, but biologists later recommended culling yearlings with fewer than 6 points. &nbsp;There was controversy\u2026\n\"Texas Books on Texas Quails\" article featured in Texas Wildlife Magazine\nTen books have been published on quails in Texas which is far more than the number of books\non quails from any other state. Texas is clearly the leading state when it comes to quail publications, which\nillustrates the important role quail play in Texas wildlife culture and economy. The purpose of this article is to share a set of thumbnail sketches on the 10 Texas based books, along with\u2026\nCKWRI research featured in documentary film \"Origins\"\nLong before quality deer management became a movement, it was an idea taking shape in the brush country of South Texas. A Huntstand original film, Origins, traces the roots of modern whitetail management back to the people, places, and ideas that changed the way America understands white-tailed deer. At the heart of the story are the pioneers who challenged conventional thinking and began\u2026\nOcelot Conservation Facility Update\nThe Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute (CKWRI) Ocelot Conservation Facility, scheduled for completion in 2026, marks a major milestone in the recovery of one of Texas' most endangered native species. Developed through a partnership among CKWRI, East Foundation, Texas A&amp;M University\u2013Kingsville, and other conservation partners, the facility will support ocelot breeding,\u2026\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nHabitat Restoration & Research\nFire Ecology\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas Research Program\nRichard M. Kleberg Jr Center for Quail Research\nQuail Associates 2.0\nSouth Texas Natives\nTexas Native Seeds\nWaterfowl and Wetland Birds\nWildlife Diseases and Toxicology\nMain navigation\nAbout\nCaesar Kleberg\nCaesar Kleberg Foundation\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nAdvisory Board\nDevelopment Team\nScience Team\nOperations Team\nGraduate Students\nProspective Students\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nFire Ecology\nHabitat Management, Restoration & Research\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock & Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nQuail Associates 2.0\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas research Program\nRichard M. Kleberg Jr Center for Quail Research\nS